The Multiplanetary Species Foundation

A Distributed Trust Infrastructure for Certification-Based Coordination Across Earth, Orbit, Moon, and Mars

Author: Ouadi Maakoul
Version: 0.4 

AI: ChatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Gemini 

Abstract

This document advances the Multiplanetary Species Foundation (MSF) research program from a preliminary institutional proposal into a theoretically grounded, methodologically disciplined doctoral research agenda. The central claim, now formulated as a falsifiable hypothesis rather than a constitutive assumption, is that a potentially binding constraint on multiplanetary coordination is the production of verifiable trust among heterogeneous actors operating under incomplete information, distributed authority, and communication delay.

Drawing on institutional economics, mechanism design theory, and comparative institutional analysis, this framework reconceptualizes MSF not as a conventional foundation but as a distributed trust infrastructure operating through a four-layer architecture: standards, verification, certification, and recognition. The document specifies the theoretical foundations through five formal propositions, identifies testable research questions with explicit falsification criteria, designs a theoretical pilot study calibrated to validate the core mechanisms without requiring physical experimentation, and outlines institutional implications strictly conditional on research success.

Critically, this version addresses the category drift between interoperability standards and safety certification, the political economy of certification under power asymmetry, and the scope constraints of a tractable doctoral research program. The document explicitly acknowledges what it does not claim: that MSF will be created, that certification solves all coordination problems, or that voluntary certification is sufficient for safety-critical systems.

