1. Introduction: The Problem of Trust in Anarchic Domains

1.1 The Coordination Challenge

Human civilization has never coordinated across a domain characterized simultaneously by:

· Latency constraints ($\tau > 0$ for all interplanetary decisions), rendering real-time central control impossible
· Distributed sovereignty among states with conflicting strategic interests
· Capital concentration in commercial entities with fiduciary obligations to shareholders
· Epistemic fragmentation across disciplines lacking shared vocabularies
· Existential risk at civilizational scale but abstract at individual scale
· Temporal horizons extending beyond electoral cycles, corporate quarters, and individual careers

Existing institutions address subsets of these conditions. None addresses their intersection. This is not an engineering problem solvable through technological advance; it is a mechanism design problem: how to construct rules, incentives, and information structures enabling rational self-interested actors to produce collectively optimal outcomes when they cannot observe, control, or fully understand each other's actions.

1.2 From Assumption to Hypothesis

Previous versions of this document asserted that "the binding constraint on multiplanetary coordination is not technology but trust." Draft 0.4 reformulates this as a testable hypothesis:

Hypothesis (H1): A potentially binding constraint on multiplanetary coordination is the production of verifiable trust among heterogeneous actors operating under incomplete information, distributed authority, and communication delay.

This formulation transforms a universal claim into a proposition that can be evaluated against evidence. The research program is designed to determine whether this hypothesis survives empirical scrutiny, and under what conditions it holds or fails.

1.3 The Trust Infrastructure Concept

The conceptual evolution from Draft 0.3 to 0.4 is substantive. MSF is no longer conceived as a single institution performing multiple functions, but as a distributed trust infrastructure that could be operated by various institutional forms. This infrastructure comprises:

\boxed{
\text{Standards} \rightarrow \text{Verification} \rightarrow \text{Certification} \rightarrow \text{Recognition} \rightarrow \text{Coordination}
}

Each layer serves a distinct function:

· Standards define what constitutes acceptable performance
· Verification determines whether evidence supports compliance claims
· Certification transforms verification into institutional assertions
· Recognition ensures certification carries economic or regulatory value
· Coordination emerges from the resulting reduction in transaction costs

The foundation, if created, would be the initial organization capable of operating this infrastructure. The infrastructure itself is the research object.

