6. Institutional Design (Strictly Conditional on Research Success)

6.1 The Distributed Trust Infrastructure Architecture

The institutional design in this section is conditional on RQ1–RQ3 yielding positive results. It should be read as a theoretical contribution to the institutional design literature, not as a plan for creating an institution.

The four-layer architecture:

\boxed{
\text{Layer 1: Standards} \rightarrow \text{Layer 2: Verification} \rightarrow \text{Layer 3: Certification} \rightarrow \text{Layer 4: Recognition}
}

Layer 1 (Standards): Open, consensus-based development of technical standards. This layer follows the IETF model: participation open to any qualified individual, standards adopted by rough consensus after open comment, standards freely available under open license.

Layer 2 (Verification): Professional, accountable verification of compliance with standards. This layer follows the UL model: professional staff with technical expertise, funded by verification fees, subject to external audit.

Layer 3 (Certification): Institutional assertion that a system meets standards. This layer transforms verification into a public claim with reputational and legal consequences.

Layer 4 (Recognition): Economic and regulatory acceptance of certification. This layer ensures certification has value:

B_i = B(C_i)

Without recognition, certification is a decorative credential. Recognition transforms certification into a coordination mechanism.

6.2 The Satellite Layer: From Communication to Trust Infrastructure

Previous drafts treated satellites as communication infrastructure. Draft 0.4 proposes a more ambitious role: satellites as components of the trust infrastructure itself.

\text{Satellite Layer} = \{ \text{Observation}, \text{Timing}, \text{Communication}, \text{Position}, \text{Data Relay}, \text{Attestation} \}

This reframing suggests a progression from "humans communicate through satellites" to "distributed systems establish verifiable states of civilization through satellite-mediated evidence."

Theoretical significance: This expands the concept beyond certification to include the infrastructure through which verification evidence is generated, transmitted, and authenticated. It connects the MSF concept to emerging work on remote sensing for compliance verification, satellite-based attestation, and space-based observation of space activities.

6.3 Governance Structure (Conditional)

General Assembly (annual):

· All institutional members
· Elects Board, approves budget, provides strategic oversight
· Voting rights tiered by contribution
· No single member or category controls majority

Board of Directors (quarterly):

· 15–21 members representing space agencies, industry, academia, civil society, independent experts
· No single category controls majority
· Conflict-of-interest disclosure and management

Technical Standards Committee (continuous):

· Open participation
· Rough consensus decision-making
· Standards adopted after public comment

Certification Division (continuous):

· Professional certification staff
· Funded by certification fees
· Technically independent from standard-setting

Appeals Board (as needed):

· Independent appointment
· Hears certification appeals

6.4 Anti-Capture Safeguards

Industry capture: Open standards process, anti-cartel constraints, tiered certification, periodic adversarial review.

Regulatory capture: Multi-jurisdictional governance, balanced board representation, funding diversification.

Certification-body capture: Staff rotation, external audit, anonymous peer review, whistleblower protection.

6.5 Funding Model (Data-Dependent)

The funding model will be determined by research findings:

· Willingness-to-pay for certification (from stakeholder interviews)
· Size of addressable market (from gap analysis)
· Cost structure of certification operations (from comparative case studies)
· Availability of philanthropic support (from fundraising feasibility assessment)

Guiding principles:

· No single funder exceeds 20% of budget
· Certification fees cover operational costs at scale
· Philanthropic support funds capacity-building and initial operations
· Data licensing provides supplementary revenue

