7.3 Methodological Approach

The research combines:

· Formal modeling: Game-theoretic and mechanism design analysis of certification
· Comparative case studies: Historical analysis of certification institutions
· Computational simulation: Agent-based modeling of certification mechanisms
· Survey research: Expert interviews, stakeholder surveys, conjoint analysis

This mixed-methods approach is appropriate for a problem spanning economics, law, and policy, with no physical experimentation or code deployment required.

7.4 What the PhD Does NOT Claim

To maintain scope discipline, the thesis explicitly does not claim:

· That MSF will be created: The research produces knowledge enabling institutional creation; it does not create the institution
· That certification solves all coordination problems: The thesis addresses specific, bounded coordination failures
· That the simulation validates the full certification theory: The simulation tests specific mechanisms; generalization requires further research
· That voluntary certification is sufficient for safety-critical systems: The thesis examines scope conditions; regulatory approaches may be necessary for high-consequence domains

