8. Addressing Criticisms of Previous Drafts

8.1 Response to "The pilot doesn't test the theory"

Draft 0.4 reframes the pilot as a theoretical exercise using simulation and survey methods rather than physical deployment. This eliminates the category drift problem while maintaining testable hypotheses.

8.2 Response to "The UL analogy is disanalogous"

Acknowledged. The comparative case studies now include failure cases (financial ratings) and explicitly assess whether certification can function for low-n, high-consequence systems. The scope conditions are treated as research findings, not assumptions.

8.3 Response to "Existing bodies could be extended"

The gap analysis now includes explicit falsification criteria. The default assumption is extension; the burden of proof is on demonstrating that a new institution is necessary.

8.4 Response to "Scope is oversized"

The scope has been narrowed substantially. RQ4–RQ6 are deferred. The pilot is theoretical, not physical. The institutional design is conditional, not assumed.

8.5 Response to "Funding numbers are decorative"

The funding model is now data-dependent, with specific empirical parameters identified as inputs.

8.6 Response to "The power asymmetry problem remains unaddressed"

Section 5 addresses power asymmetry qualitatively. The geopolitical fragmentation scenario is treated as a central research concern, not a background condition.

