Conclusion: From Certification Theory to Distributed Trust Infrastructure

Draft 0.4 represents a substantial revision addressing the major criticisms of earlier drafts. The central claim is now formulated as a falsifiable hypothesis rather than a constitutive assumption. The four-layer architecture (standards, verification, certification, recognition) provides a more precise specification of the mechanisms through which certification enables coordination. The theoretical framework generates five formal propositions with clear empirical implications. The pilot study has been redesigned as a theoretical exercise using simulation and survey methods appropriate to a doctoral research program.

The central hypothesis remains: a potentially binding constraint on multiplanetary coordination is the production of verifiable trust among heterogeneous actors operating under incomplete information, distributed authority, and communication delay. Whether this hypothesis survives empirical scrutiny remains to be determined. The research program is designed to find out.

The path from Earth to Mars is long. The path from certification theory to institutional practice is equally long. Both begin with a single, well-defined step. This document describes that step: a theoretically grounded, empirically testable, scope-disciplined research program that either validates the certification approach or identifies the conditions under which it fails.

