Appendix A: Complete Mathematical Proofs

A.1 Proof of Proposition P1 (Information)

Proposition P1: Certification reduces uncertainty about latent system properties:

H(S_i | C_i) < H(S_i)

where $H$ denotes informational entropy.

A.1.1 Setup

Let $S_i \in \{s_L, s_H\}$ be a binary latent safety type with $s_L < s_H$. The prior distribution is:

\Pr(S_i = s_H) = p, \quad \Pr(S_i = s_L) = 1-p

The certifier observes a signal:

\hat{s}_i = s_i + \varepsilon_i, \quad \varepsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_\varepsilon^2)

and issues certificate:

c_i = \begin{cases} 
1 & \text{if } \hat{s}_i \geq \theta \\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}

A.1.2 Derivation of Posterior

The probability of certification given type $s$ is:

\Pr(c_i = 1 | S_i = s) = \Pr(\hat{s}_i \geq \theta | S_i = s) = \Pr(s + \varepsilon_i \geq \theta) = \Phi\left(\frac{s - \theta}{\sigma_\varepsilon}\right)

where $\Phi$ is the standard normal CDF.

Define:

q_H = \Phi\left(\frac{s_H - \theta}{\sigma_\varepsilon}\right), \quad q_L = \Phi\left(\frac{s_L - \theta}{\sigma_\varepsilon}\right)

By Bayes' rule, the posterior probability of high type given certification is:

\Pr(S_i = s_H | c_i = 1) = \frac{p \cdot q_H}{p \cdot q_H + (1-p) \cdot q_L}

and given non-certification:

\Pr(S_i = s_H | c_i = 0) = \frac{p \cdot (1-q_H)}{p \cdot (1-q_H) + (1-p) \cdot (1-q_L)}

A.1.3 Entropy Comparison

The prior entropy is:

H(S_i) = -p \ln p - (1-p) \ln(1-p)

The posterior entropy given certification status is:

H(S_i | C_i) = \Pr(c_i = 1) \cdot H(S_i | c_i=1) + \Pr(c_i = 0) \cdot H(S_i | c_i=0)

where:

\Pr(c_i = 1) = p \cdot q_H + (1-p) \cdot q_L

A.1.4 Proof that $H(S_i | C_i) < H(S_i)$

Theorem A.1: For any signal with $q_H \neq q_L$, conditioning on the signal strictly reduces entropy:

H(S_i | C_i) < H(S_i)

Proof:

This is a standard result in information theory (Cover & Thomas, 2006, Theorem 2.6.5). The mutual information between $S_i$ and $C_i$ is:

I(S_i; C_i) = H(S_i) - H(S_i | C_i)

The mutual information is non-negative and equals zero if and only if $S_i$ and $C_i$ are independent. Since $q_H \neq q_L$ (the signal is informative about type), $S_i$ and $C_i$ are not independent, so $I(S_i; C_i) > 0$, hence:

H(S_i | C_i) = H(S_i) - I(S_i; C_i) < H(S_i)

∎

A.1.5 Comparative Statics

Corollary A.1: The information value of certification decreases as signal noise increases:

\frac{\partial I(S_i; C_i)}{\partial \sigma_\varepsilon} < 0

Proof sketch: As $\sigma_\varepsilon \to \infty$, $q_H \to q_L$, so the signal becomes uninformative. As $\sigma_\varepsilon \to 0$, $q_H \to 1$ and $q_L \to 0$ (assuming $\theta \in (s_L, s_H)$), so the signal perfectly reveals type. By continuity, mutual information is monotonically decreasing in $\sigma_\varepsilon$.

Corollary A.2: The information value of certification is maximized when the threshold is set at:

\theta^* = \frac{s_H + s_L}{2}

Proof: The mutual information is maximized when the signal maximally separates the two types. Given symmetric noise, this occurs when the threshold is equidistant from both type means.

