Appendix B: Agent-Based Simulation Design

B.1 Overview

The agent-based simulation tests Propositions P1–P4 in a controlled computational environment. The simulation models $N$ heterogeneous actors choosing safety investment levels under different certification regimes.

B.2 Model Specification

B.2.1 Agent Types

Each agent $i$ is characterized by:

· Risk aversion: $\alpha_i \sim \text{Uniform}(0.5, 2.0)$
· Cost parameter: $\beta_i \sim \text{Lognormal}(0, 0.5)$
· Baseline accident risk: $r_i^0 \sim \text{Uniform}(0.01, 0.20)$
· Market value of certification: $B_i \sim \text{Lognormal}(1, 0.5)$

B.2.2 Safety Investment and Accident Probability

Agent $i$ chooses safety investment $s_i \in [0, 10]$. The accident probability is:

\Pr(\text{accident}_i) = r_i^0 \cdot \exp(-\beta_i s_i)

The expected accident cost is:

R_i(s_i) = \alpha_i \cdot r_i^0 \cdot \exp(-\beta_i s_i) \cdot L

where $L = 100$ is the loss given accident (normalized units).

B.2.3 Certification Mechanism

The certifier observes:

\hat{s}_i = s_i + \varepsilon_i, \quad \varepsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_\varepsilon^2)

and issues certificate if $\hat{s}_i \geq \theta$, where $\theta$ is the certification threshold.

The certification benefit is:

B_i^{cert} = B_i \cdot \mathbb{1}(c_i = 1)

B.2.4 Externalities

Actor $i$'s accident probability depends on others' safety investments through:

\Pr(\text{accident}_i) = r_i^0 \cdot \exp(-\beta_i s_i) \cdot \exp\left(-\delta \sum_{j \neq i} s_j\right)

where $\delta = 0.01$ captures the externality strength.

B.3 Simulation Parameters

Parameter Description Range Baseline
$N$ Number of actors 10–100 50
$\sigma_\varepsilon$ Verification noise 0.5–5.0 2.0
$\theta$ Certification threshold 2–8 5
$B$ Mean certification benefit 1–20 10
$\delta$ Externality strength 0–0.05 0.01
$\alpha$ Risk aversion 0.5–2.0 1.0

B.4 Experimental Design

B.4.1 Treatments

1. No certification (baseline): Agents choose $s_i$ without certification mechanism
2. Weak certification: Low $B$, high $\sigma_\varepsilon$
3. Moderate certification: Moderate $B$, moderate $\sigma_\varepsilon$
4. Strong certification: High $B$, low $\sigma_\varepsilon$
5. Certification with capture: Certifier has probability $\rho$ of certifying regardless of actual $s_i$

B.4.2 Outcome Variables

· Mean safety investment: $\bar{s} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_i s_i$
· Certification rate: $\frac{1}{N} \sum_i c_i$
· Welfare: $W(\mathbf{s})$ as defined in Section 2.2
· Certification accuracy: $\Pr(c_i = 1 | s_i \geq \theta)$
· False certification rate: $\Pr(c_i = 1 | s_i < \theta)$

B.4.3 Hypotheses (Pre-registered)

· H1: Mean safety investment is higher under certification than baseline
· H2: Certification rate increases with $B$ and decreases with $\sigma_\varepsilon$
· H3: Welfare is higher under certification than baseline
· H4: Certification effectiveness degrades with capture ($\rho > 0$)
· H5: Certification generates positive externalities (mean safety investment of non-certified actors is higher when others are certified)

B.5 Implementation

B.5.1 Software

The simulation will be implemented in Python using Mesa (an agent-based modeling framework) or NetLogo. Parameter sweeps will be conducted using Latin Hypercube Sampling for efficient exploration of parameter space.

B.5.2 Validation

The simulation will be validated by:

1. Face validity: Comparison with theoretical predictions
2. Sensitivity analysis: Systematic variation of parameters
3. Replication: Multiple runs with different random seeds

B.5.3 Limitations

The simulation is a simplified representation of the space domain. It does not capture:

· Strategic interaction (agents are assumed to be non-strategic)
· Learning and adaptation
· Reputational dynamics
· Regulatory complexity

These limitations will be acknowledged and their implications discussed in the thesis.

