Carbon credits Environment Hybrid Mixed

C.2 Analytical Framework

C.2.1 Variables

For each case, the following variables will be coded:

· Volume: Number of certified entities per year
· Consequence: Severity of certification failure
· Technical maturity: Age of technology at certification emergence
· Insurance integration: Whether certification is required for insurance
· Regulatory recognition: Whether certification is recognized by regulators
· Capture risk: Whether certification has been subject to capture
· Emergence mechanism: How certification emerged (market demand, regulatory mandate, hybrid)

C.2.2 Coding Procedure

Each case will be coded by the researcher and independently verified by a second coder. Disagreements will be resolved through discussion.

C.3 Comparative Analysis

C.3.1 Method

The analysis will use:

· Qualitative comparative analysis (QCA): Identify necessary and sufficient conditions for certification success
· Process tracing: Identify causal mechanisms in individual cases
· Cross-case comparison: Identify patterns across cases

C.3.2 Key Questions

1. Did certification emerge in domains with space-like characteristics?
2. What conditions are necessary for certification success?
3. What conditions are sufficient for certification failure?
4. Can these conditions be mapped to the space domain?

C.4 Expected Findings

Based on preliminary analysis, the following patterns are expected:

1. Certification succeeds when volume is high and consequences are moderate
2. Certification fails when consequences are catastrophic and volume is low
3. Sovereign certification is more common in high-consequence domains
4. Private certification requires insurance integration or regulatory recognition

These patterns will be tested against the space domain in Chapter 5 of the thesis.

