Appendix P: Statement of Original Contribution

P.1 Contribution to Knowledge

This thesis makes the following original contributions:

P.1.1 Theoretical Contribution

A formal model of certification as a coordination mechanism in domains lacking sovereign authority, with five propositions specifying the conditions under which certification:

1. Reduces information asymmetry (P1)
2. Induces safety investment (P2)
3. Generates trust (P3)
4. Reduces transaction costs (P4)
5. Becomes institutionally viable (P5)

P.1.2 Empirical Contribution

A systematic comparison of certification institutions across five domains (aviation, maritime, nuclear, medical devices, financial ratings) identifying:

· Scope conditions for certification success
· Failure modes of certification
· Transferable design principles

P.1.3 Methodological Contribution

An agent-based simulation framework for testing certification mechanisms, calibrated to the space domain, with pre-registered hypotheses and falsification criteria.

P.1.4 Policy Contribution

A framework for evaluating whether certification gaps exist in space governance and whether extension of existing institutions or creation of new institutions is warranted.

P.2 Limitations

The thesis acknowledges the following limitations:

1. Theoretical: The model treats actors symmetrically; extension to asymmetric cases is future work
2. Empirical: The simulation is a simplified representation; generalization requires further research
3. Methodological: The case studies are subject to selection bias; the inclusion of failure cases mitigates but does not eliminate this
4. Policy: The institutional design is conditional on research success; it should not be read as a plan for creation

P.3 Future Research

Future research should address:

1. Asymmetric actor models
2. Dynamic certification with learning and adaptation
3. Interplanetary certification under latency
4. Automated certification using formal methods
5. Empirical validation through physical deployment

